AI Test Tube Racking Robot
I'm building an automatic test tube racking robot to help scientists save time in labs. Instead of people having to move hundreds of tubes by hand, my robot uses a cheap robotic arm and an AI camera to grab messy test tubes out of a box and line them up perfectly into a rack.
How It Works
The Hardware: I'm building the frame out of aluminum extrusions and clear acrylic sheets. I'm 3D printing a bunch of custom parts and funnels to route the tubes, and using an ESP32-CAM and standard servos to move the arm.
The Brains: To train the AI, I’m physically moving the arm by hand to show it what to do, and also using NVIDIA Isaac Sim to get extra training data. I'm writing it in Python using Hugging Face libraries to get the computer vision model working. I will probably use ESP32 aswell.
The Action: Tubes get dumped into a funnel to slide into the pick zone. The camera detects the tube, figures out the best angle to grab it, and the arm picks it up and drops it right into a slot in the rack.
Current Progress
Right now, I’m mostly sourcing parts and starting to build the main frame. (I 3d printed them on a bambu lab x1 carbon) Next, I need to order all of the parts.




